WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.982
Weaviate supports several ways to perform vector search.

00:00:01.982 --> 00:00:03.672
In the example, the near_text pattern is useful when the collection is configured with a vectorizer.

00:00:03.672 --> 00:00:05.932
You provide natural-language text, and the system handles the vectorization before searching.

00:00:05.932 --> 00:00:09.752
If your application generates its own embeddings, you can instead use a near-vector style query.

00:00:09.752 --> 00:00:10.222
This distinction is worth noticing.

00:00:10.222 --> 00:00:12.292
[tts-break] Some platforms want the application to own embedding generation.

00:00:12.292 --> 00:00:15.712
Others can integrate the embedding step into the database workflow.

00:00:15.712 --> 00:00:16.092
Neither approach is automatically better.

00:00:16.092 --> 00:00:18.942
The choice affects consistency, model upgrades, cost, and operational control.

00:00:18.942 --> 00:00:21.762
When you evaluate a vector platform, ask who creates the embeddings, where the model configuration lives,

00:00:21.762 --> 00:00:22.312
how metadata filters work, and how the system handles updates.

00:00:22.312 --> 00:00:24.235
Those questions matter more than the exact method names in the SDK.
